Here is a concise, zero-dependency Rust function to check if a number is prime:

```rust
fn is_prime(n: u64) -> bool {
    if n < 2 {
        return false;
    }
    if n == 2 {
        return true;
    }
    if n % 2 == 0 {
        return false;
    }
    let mut i = 3;
    while i * i <= n {
        if n % i == 0 {
            return false;
        }
        i += 2;
    }
    true
}
```

This function works by checking if `n` is less than 2 (which is not prime), then checking if it's even (which means it's not prime unless it's 2). Finally, it iterates through odd numbers up to the square root of `n` to see if any divide it evenly.
